---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DiffCheckerCode from '@/components/tools/dev/DiffCheckerCode.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="diff-checker-code">
  <DiffCheckerCode client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to compare two versions of a file</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste the original on the left and the changed version on the right. Give each a file name if you will export a patch.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Unified</strong> for a git-style view with both sets of line numbers, or <strong>Side by side</strong> to see the two files aligned.</li>
      <li>Turn on the ignore options to filter out reformatting noise. Trailing whitespace is ignored by default, because it is almost never a real change.</li>
      <li>Copy the unified patch, or download it as a <code>.patch</code> file for <code>git apply</code>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Unified or side-by-side?</h2>
    <p>
      Unified is denser and matches the view in a pull request or a terminal, so it is better for
      reviewing a change you already understand. Side by side is better when lines were rewritten, not
      added or removed: you read the before and after on one row and do not need to look for the matching
      pair a screen away. Both views show line numbers for both files, so you can quote a finding
      precisely.
    </p>

    <h2>How the diff is computed</h2>
    <p>
      The comparison uses the Myers O(ND) algorithm over lines, from the same family as the git default.
      It finds the longest common subsequence of the two files, which is the diff with the fewest
      inserted and deleted lines. For this reason the hunks match your version control, and the patch
      output applies cleanly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code diff checker runs in your browser, so you can compare proprietary or unreleased source
      without sending it to a server.
    </p>

    <h2>The ignore options</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th><th>When to use it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Trailing whitespace</td><td>Removes spaces and tabs at the end of a line before comparing</td><td>Almost always: editors add and remove these invisibly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Leading whitespace</td><td>Removes indentation before comparing</td><td>After a re-indent, or when code moved into a new block</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repeated spaces and tabs</td><td>Collapses runs of whitespace to one space</td><td>After a formatter changed alignment padding</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letter case</td><td>Compares case-insensitively</td><td>Comparing SQL, config keys or generated identifiers</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The ignore options affect only matching. The lines shown, copied and exported are always the
      originals, so a patch made with whitespace ignored still contains the real bytes.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading a unified patch</h2>
    <p>
      A unified diff is made of hunks, each with a header such as <code>@@ -12,7 +12,9 @@</code>. This
      means: from line 12 of the original, seven lines are covered; from line 12 of the new file, nine
      lines are covered. In the hunk, a leading space marks an unchanged context line, <code>-</code> a
      removed line and <code>+</code> an added one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Context lines make a patch robust. Three lines on each side (the git default) let the patch apply
      even if the surrounding file moved a little. Zero context gives the smallest and most fragile patch;
      ten is useful when a reviewer must see the whole function.
    </p>

    <h2>Applying the patch</h2>
    <p>
      Download the file, then run <code>git apply changes.patch</code> from the repository root. If the
      paths in the header do not match your working tree, change the file-name fields above before you
      export, or apply with <code>patch -p1 &lt; changes.patch</code>. <code>git apply --check</code> tells
      you if the patch will apply cleanly, without changes to any file.
    </p>

    <h2>Which diff tool do you want?</h2>
    <p>
      This page compares lines and is made for source code. For prose, where one paragraph is one very
      long line and you want to see changed words, the text diff checker gives a much more readable
      result. For structured data, format both sides with the JSON formatter (with key sorting on) before
      the diff, so ordering differences do not hide the real change.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
